# ╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
# ║  LOCAL ONLY — the same topology as deploy/Caddyfile, on `localhost`,       ║
# ║  with a certificate from Caddy's own internal CA instead of from ACME.     ║
# ║                                                                           ║
# ║  Reachable only through `docker-compose.dev.yml`. Do not copy this over    ║
# ║  deploy/Caddyfile: a certificate nobody outside this machine trusts is     ║
# ║  exactly wrong for a public deployment, and `localhost` is not a site      ║
# ║  address anybody can reach.                                               ║
# ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
#
# Until #40 this file served cleartext HTTP on `:80` with `auto_https off`, on
# the argument that "a self-signed certificate on localhost teaches nobody
# anything about whether the production path works". That argument was wrong in
# a way the deployment then demonstrated: with a cleartext origin, `app` refuses
# to start at all (`packages/auth/src/transport.ts`), so the local stack could
# not run the web application — the half of the topology most worth running
# locally. Cleartext here did not teach less about production; it made the local
# stack a different product.
#
# So this file is now the production file with two differences, both forced by
# the hostname rather than chosen:
#
#  1. the site address is `localhost`, which Caddy serves over HTTPS using the
#     internal CA it manages itself — no ACME, no public DNS, no rate limits;
#  2. no HSTS header, because pinning `localhost` to HTTPS for a year in every
#     browser on the machine is a thing to do to a real hostname, not to the one
#     every other local project also uses.
#
# Everything else — the `/ws` and `/health` routing, the `reverse_proxy`
# behaviour the rate limiter's IP dimension depends on — is identical, so
# exercising it here exercises the arrangement production runs.

{
	admin off
	# Where Caddy's local root lands, for `docker compose cp`:
	#   /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt
}

localhost {
	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}

	handle /ws* {
		reverse_proxy server:4000
	}
	handle /health {
		reverse_proxy server:4000
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy app:3000
	}
}

# The proxy's own liveness, on the loopback interface of its container only.
#
# Bound to `localhost` rather than `:2019`, so Caddy listens on 127.0.0.1 and
# nothing on the compose network — let alone off the box — can reach it. It
# exists because a container health check has to ask *this process* whether it
# is serving, and every other answer available (`caddy validate`, a TCP probe)
# proves something weaker than "the HTTP server is up and routing".
http://localhost:2019 {
	handle /caddy-health {
		respond "ok" 200
	}
	handle {
		respond 404
	}
}
